# Nothing in this file is needed to run learn.ninja locally: `npm install && npm run dev` keeps lessons
# in PGlite, counts rate limits in memory, and prints sign-in codes and feedback to the server console.
# These are for a hosted deployment, or to try one integration on your machine.
#
# Copy to apps/web/.env.local and fill in. Never commit real values.

# Lesson storage: a Postgres connection string (production uses Neon over HTTP).
# The web app's build applies migrations to it (packages/db/drizzle). Without it, development keeps
# lessons in PGlite at apps/web/.data/pglite; set LEARN_PGLITE_DIR to use another folder, or to run
# a production build without a database (the browser tests do).
# DATABASE_URL=
# LEARN_PGLITE_DIR=

# Rate limits. Without these, development counts in memory.
# On Vercel, adding Upstash Redis from the Marketplace sets KV_REST_API_URL and KV_REST_API_TOKEN instead,
# which also work.
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=

# The bearer token Vercel Cron sends to /api/cron/expire, which deletes expired lessons daily.
# Any long random string; without it the route refuses every request.
# CRON_SECRET=

# Optional: the public origin used in published links (defaults to the request's origin).
# LEARN_PUBLIC_URL=https://learn.ninja

# Accounts (Better Auth). Sessions are signed with this secret: any long random string, e.g.
# `openssl rand -base64 32`. Every hosted deployment needs it, and deployments that share a database
# must use the same value: it also encrypts the private key that signs the OAuth tokens chat apps get,
# in the database's jwks table, so a key minted under another secret leaves the others unable to sign
# (changing it signs every chat app out). Development uses a default.
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=
# Optional: a fixed test account for app-store reviewers: this address always signs in with this
# six-digit code and gets no email. Set both only while a review is open, then unset them.
# REVIEW_SIGN_IN_EMAIL=
# REVIEW_SIGN_IN_CODE=
# Optional: the site's origin for sign-in callbacks (defaults to the request's host, which is right
# for learn.ninja, preview deployments, and localhost alike).
# BETTER_AUTH_URL=https://learn.ninja

# Sign in with GitHub: a GitHub OAuth app with the callback URL {your origin}/api/auth/callback/github. Without these, the sign-in page only offers email codes.
# GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
# GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Sign-in codes by email, through Resend (https://resend.com) from EMAIL_FROM. Without the key,
# codes are printed to the server console instead.
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# EMAIL_FROM=Your name <you@example.com>

# Where reports on lessons go (the Report item in a listed lesson's menu), sent through Resend from
# EMAIL_FROM. Without it, reports are kept in the database and printed to the server console.
# REPORT_EMAIL=you@example.com

# Where the header's bug button sends feedback: a Slack incoming webhook URL
# (https://api.slack.com/messaging/webhooks) for a feedback channel. Without it, feedback is
# printed to the server console.
# SLACK_FEEDBACK_WEBHOOK_URL=https://hooks.slack.com/services/…

# The ninja's voice: an ElevenLabs (https://elevenlabs.io) API key for `npm run voice -w
# @workspace/player`, which makes his spoken lines on your machine. Only that script reads it; the
# site never calls ElevenLabs, so Vercel doesn't need it.
# ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=

# A GitHub token for the mission page's contributor wall, which reads the repository's committers
# once a day. Optional: without it the call is unauthenticated and shares GitHub's limit of 60
# requests an hour per IP with every other build on the same machine. Any fine-grained token with
# public read access works; it needs no scopes.
# GITHUB_TOKEN=

# The iOS app's Apple Developer Team ID (10 characters, from developer.apple.com > Membership), so
# /.well-known/apple-app-site-association names the app (ninja.learn.ios) and learn.ninja's lesson
# and profile links open in it. Without it the file answers 404 and links open in Safari.
# APPLE_TEAM_ID=
